  [PASS] C1.AB4 THE TRANSPORT TWIST IS NOT D65's COBOUNDARY AND NORMALISATION DOES NOT REMOVE IT.  D65's repair — divide by the per-state menu mass — is the corpus's one committed repair for an exchange defect.  Applied here it leaves every one of the raw-defective squares EXACTLY where it was, because the intermediate mass ratio is identically 1 on all of them, and it ADDS a DISJOINT family of new defects which are ratios of menu masses.  The two twists live on disjoint square sets and their values share no prime beyond 2: the raw twist is 3-smooth, the mass twist is not  (mass ratio on the raw-defective squares = {'1': 88} (so r is UNCHANGED by normalisation there); surviving 3-smooth defects {'1/2': 70, '2': 10, '2/3': 2, '3/2': 6} = the raw spectrum {'1/2': 70, '2': 10, '2/3': 2, '3/2': 6}; new non-3-smooth defects {'4/5': 114, '5/4': 90}, every one a ratio of two realised menu masses; D65's committed ratio set is ['1', '4/5', '5/4'] and the AB4 mass twist REPRODUCES it exactly)
